mod:pointer_returned_holder;
// 2026-11-12 (landing 177d2b5, row 4: DEF-591's getter road, and DEF-654). A POINTER A GETTER RETURNED, HANDED STRAIGHT TO A WRITER, IS A WRITE TO THE HOLDER THE
// GETTER WAS HANDED, AND A HEADER'S `.ptr` HANDED TO A CALLEE THAT WRITES THROUGH IT IS A WRITE OF THE STORAGE IT POINTS INTO.
// In `mid(H->:w) { pass raw growl(raw getl(w)); }` with `getl` = `pass @w.l;` the writer's argument is a call's result: it has no place, and its targets are read
// off the call's provenance. Landing 177d2b4's row 6 wrote such a target only where its type was the pointer's own or its pointee's (`pointer_returned_target.npk`), so
// `mid` said nothing of `w`, and its caller kept a pointer into the `List` across the growth. Now, where the target's type is neither:
//   - a struct the program declares, reached by a pointer neither crossed nor retyped, is written BY FIELD: each field that is or holds the pointer's pointee type
//     (a slice field holds its element type, through its run);
//   - any other holder is written WHOLE: an array, a slice (at its elements), an optional, an enum, a `List`, an open type, a pointer below the place, and any holder
//     at all when the pointer was crossed by `=>! wild` or retyped on its road (its claimed pointee type is no evidence of what it points at);
//   - a pointer neither crossed nor retyped whose pointee type the holder doesn't hold writes nothing: it can't point into it;
//   - a header's own pointer (a member read off a string, a cstring, a slice, a buffer or a `List`, under any cast) writes nothing only at a call that names a
//     builtin or an extern. Handed to any other callee it is written as any other pointer is.
// One cell a face, its names suffixed with the cell's. Each face was accepted before this row and read freed memory: 236 (or 235, the `gs_` cells) at -O0 and -O2
// on roads 0 to 2 and 97 (MachineFault) under the freed-heap detector, on b510126 (177d2b3), on main (169d6d4) and on row 3's commit (a633585). BORROW-015 each now.
// DEF-591'S FIVE (rulings "177d2b5's first rows"; the cells are the attack's): `t1` (the getter road itself), `t3`, `t5`, `t6`, `fv5`.
// THE SEVEN SPELLINGS (nitpick-compiler_103's cells, job B): the getter's pointer kept somewhere other than a plain local before the write -- `t1s` (a struct's
// field), `t1a` (an array element), `t1d` (behind a pointer), `t1sp` (through a pointer to the struct), `t1o` (through an Optional), `t1f` (a member off a call's
// result), `t1ll` (a `List` of `List`s: the path ends at a header).
// And `t1two` (two fields of the pointee's type: the getter hands the second, and the held address is in it), `t1deep` (the pointee's type two structs down).
// THE COMPILER'S OWN SHAPE (nitpick-compiler_103's, job B): `gs_t1`, `gs_t2`, `gs_t3` are `emit_program.npk`'s getter-road sites with the held field written.
// THE HOLDERS THE TEXT WRITES WHOLE BY READING (nitpick-compiler_112's cells, row 4; rulings l.7701, item 2, and l.8377, item 1):
//   an optional or an enum holder -- `o_optx`, `o_enux` (the getter reaches the payload through a mirror struct, the pointer retyped through `any->`: no plain
//   spelling takes an address inside either), `o_enum`, `o_optm` (the writer takes the mirror itself);
//   an open `T` -- `o_gen` (`mid<T: Lk>(T->:w)`, the getter a trait method), `o_genb` (a generic struct `Bx<T>`);
//   a pointer retyped or crossed on its road -- `o_xr` (retyped to a type the holder holds), `o_xw` (crossed by `wild`, the pointee kept), `o_xr2` (retyped to a
//   layout twin the holder doesn't hold), `o_xw2` (the same, crossed by `wild`), `o_xr3` (the retype at the call, over a plain getter's result), `o_xr4` (the retype
//   by a second function between the getter and the writer); and `o_xr2u` (management_40's: `o_xr2`'s holder with the un-retyped getter's result passed straight);
//   an array holder -- `o_arr` (`H[2]`, the getter `@((<-w)[0i64].l)`);
//   the getter's own shape -- `o_late` (declared after its caller), `o_rec` (recursive), `o_mrec` (two getters, mutually recursive).
// `t1f_tw` (made by nitpick-compiler_112's twin rule; rulings l.8461, item 8): `t1f` with the getter's body written inside `mkl`. `mid` still reads the pointer off
// the call's result, so it is the row's road again and no local-road twin.
// A SLICE AS THE HOLDER (DEF-591's slice holder; Ailp-team_112's cells, the row's attack; rulings l.8461): the getter is handed a slice's address and hands back an
// element's, and before this row a slice was no target of such a pointer --
//   `b_sget2` (a slice parameter's address), `b_sget2e` (one call deeper), `e_sloc` (a slice local over an array field), `i_hsgv` (a slice field's element),
//   `j_slc` (a slice of structs), `j_slst` (a slice local over a `List` behind a pointer), `j_ssub` (a slice local over the parameter's own run), `j_sptrg` (the
//   slice's own `.ptr` cast to its element's pointer type in the getter);
//   the slice's address crossed on its road -- `m_cast1` (through `any->` and back), `m_castw` (through `wild` and back);
//   the address or the slice kept in a local first -- `q2_lps` (a local pointer to the parameter), `q2_csl`, `q2_ssl` (a call's slice kept in a local), `q2_fs` (a
//   slice field's address);
//   inside ONE function, the view and the growth both through a slice local of holders -- `r2_own`, `r2_own2`, `r2_ownl`;
//   the slice itself the target, at the EXACT end -- `x_sid` (nitpick-compiler_113's; rulings l.8629): an identity function hands back the pointer to the slice
//   (`pass sp;`) and the callee grows an element through it. The write is the slice's every element, where the empty path named the binding and recorded nothing.
// A HEADER'S `.ptr` HANDED TO A CALLEE THAT WRITES THROUGH IT (DEF-654; nitpick-compiler_112's `o_hp` cells and Ailp-team_112's; rulings l.8377, item 4, and
// l.8461, item 2). The pointer is cast to the element's pointer type (`(s.ptr) =>! List<int64>->`), and the callee grows the `List` it points at --
//   a slice's own `.ptr`: `o_hps` (a slice parameter's), `o_hp2`, `k_hsptr` (a slice field's), `o_hpg2`, `l_hsptrg` (through a getter), `k_spptr` (through a pointer
//   to the slice), `l_csptr` (a call's slice);
//   a range's `.ptr`: of a slice (`j_srng`), of a `List` (`f_rng`, `g_lrng`, `k_lcrng`, `k_nrng`; `j_rr`, a range of a range), of an array (`g_arng`; `j_arngw`,
//   crossed by `wild` too);
//   handed straight to the prelude's `list_push` (`n_bpush`, `n_bpusha`, `n_bpushh`, `n_bpushl`, `n_bpushp`, `n_bpushs`) or `list_reserve` (`n_bres`);
//   handed to a method through `dyn` (`u_dyn`) or through a generic's bound (`u_gen`);
//   handed to a call that names no function: a function value (`u_fv`, `u_fvs`), a callback parameter (`u_cb`), a user function's variadic position (`u_var`).
// RIGHT PROGRAMS THE ROW REFUSES, AT PARITY (each one's twin with the pointer taken in the caller was refused before the row): `gs_t4` (job B's: the held
// field written by the call whose argument read its length), `gs_t5` (written after the last read): neither can fault as written and both ran 67; `o_gen_oth_ok`
// (an open `T`, the view in another member; its local-pointer twin `o_gen_othl_ok` is refused on main).
// RIGHT PROGRAMS THE ROW REFUSES, NOT AT PARITY (DEF-655, LOW, OPEN; rulings l.8377, item 2, and l.8461, item 4): a pointer the arm can't place is written at every
// part of its holder that holds its pointee type, so a view in ANOTHER part is refused. Each is refused at the call or the store, never with a wrong run, and each
// has a twin that every build accepts (they stand in `pointer_returned_holder_ok.npk`):
//   `o_arr1_oth_ok` (`H[2]`, the view in the other element), `o_xr_oth_ok` (retyped to a type the holder holds), `o_xw_oth_ok` (crossed by `wild`), `o_xr2_oth_ok`
//   (management_40's: retyped to a type the holder doesn't hold); their twins take the pointer in the caller as the getter takes it;
//   `a_two_oth_ok`, `d_price2_ok` (Ailp-team_112's: two fields of the pointee's type, the view in the other one); their twins take the address at the call;
//   `m_sscal_ok` (a getter's pointer to one slice element's scalar field is a write to every element whole), `p_lk_ok` (a slice local is taken whole as the target
//   of such a pointer); their twins take the address by index;
//   THE WRONG OBJECT -- `s_ext_ok`, `s_exti_ok` (a slice of holders), `s_extp_ok`, `s_extpi_ok` (a pointer to one): the holder has its own `List` and, beside it, a
//   pointer of the same pointee type to another `List`. A growth through that pointer, read out of the holder in the function it was handed to, is taken as a
//   write of the holder's own `List`. Their twins write the same growth in the caller.
//   `k_ptrb_ok` (nitpick-compiler_114's; rulings l.8643) is the wrong object one getter deeper: the holder's pointer member points at another struct, and a getter
//   takes that struct's `List`. The growth is taken by its type as a write of the holder's own `List<int64>`. Its twin `k_ptrb_i_ok` writes it in the caller.
// A RIGHT PROGRAM THE ROW REFUSES (DEF-656, LOW, OPEN; rulings l.8461, item 4): `m_wrapw_ok`. A function that hands a builtin its pointer parameter is a writer
// through it (a builtin is an unknown callee), so a header's `.ptr` handed to that wrapper is a write of the storage it points into. Its twin calls the builtin
// in `mid` itself and is accepted.
// The prices move to the _ok file when their rows land.
// STILL OPEN, and in no cell here: a pointer field below the place (`t1hp`, DEF-350's shape, at DEF-312's landing); the retyped pointer kept in a plain local
// (DEF-357); a getter's pointer into a slice that a local holds (DEF-657); a write through a slice parameter's address crossed by a pointee-changing cast (DEF-658).
error:E5;
struct:K_t1 = { int64:k; };
struct:H_t1 = { List<int64>:l; int64:k; K_t1:kk; };
func:mk_t1 = List<int64>() never fails { List<int64>:l = raw list_init::<int64>(1i64); drop list_push(@l, 1i64); pass l; };
func:mkh_t1 = H_t1() never fails { pass H_t1{ l: raw mk_t1(), k: 2i64, kk: K_t1{ k: 3i64 } }; };
func:growh_t1 = int64(H_t1->:w) never fails { loop(0i64, 64i64, 1i64) { drop list_push(@w.l, 1i64); } pass 7i64; };
func:growl_t1 = int64(List<int64>->:l) never fails { loop(0i64, 64i64, 1i64) { drop list_push(l, 1i64); } pass 7i64; };
func:fp_t1 = int64->(H_t1->:q) never fails { pass @((<-q).l[0i64]); };
func:idh_t1 = H_t1->(H_t1->:q) never fails { pass q; };
func:getl_t1 = List<int64>->(H_t1->:w) never fails { pass @w.l; };
struct:HPO_t1 = { H_t1->:lp; List<int64>:own; };
func:mid_t1 = int64(H_t1->:w) never fails { pass raw growl_t1(raw getl_t1(w)); };
func:g_t1 = int64(H_t1->:h0p, H_t1->:hq, H_t1->:hz, int64:n) never fails {
    int64->:e = raw fp_t1(hq);
    discard(raw mid_t1(hq));
    pass (<-e) + 6i64;
};
func:f_t1 = int64(int64:n) never fails {
    H_t1:hz = raw mkh_t1(); H_t1:h0 = raw mkh_t1(); H_t1:h2 = raw mkh_t1();
    pass raw g_t1(@h0, @h2, @hz, n);
};
func:cell_main_t1 = int32() never fails { pass (((raw f_t1(1i64)) + 60i64) =>! int32); };
struct:K_t3 = { int64:k; };
struct:H_t3 = { List<int64>:l; int64:k; K_t3:kk; };
func:mk_t3 = List<int64>() never fails { List<int64>:l = raw list_init::<int64>(1i64); drop list_push(@l, 1i64); pass l; };
func:mkh_t3 = H_t3() never fails { pass H_t3{ l: raw mk_t3(), k: 2i64, kk: K_t3{ k: 3i64 } }; };
func:growh_t3 = int64(H_t3->:w) never fails { loop(0i64, 64i64, 1i64) { drop list_push(@w.l, 1i64); } pass 7i64; };
func:growl_t3 = int64(List<int64>->:l) never fails { loop(0i64, 64i64, 1i64) { drop list_push(l, 1i64); } pass 7i64; };
func:fp_t3 = int64->(H_t3->:q) never fails { pass @((<-q).l[0i64]); };
func:idh_t3 = H_t3->(H_t3->:q) never fails { pass q; };
func:getl_t3 = List<int64>->(H_t3->:w) never fails { pass @w.l; };
struct:HPO_t3 = { H_t3->:lp; List<int64>:own; };
struct:H2_t3 = { H_t3:h; int64:z; };
func:geth_t3 = H_t3->(H2_t3->:w) never fails { pass @((<-w).h); };
func:mid_t3 = int64(H2_t3->:w) never fails { pass raw growh_t3(raw geth_t3(w)); };
func:g_t3 = int64(H_t3->:h0p, H_t3->:hq, H_t3->:hz, int64:n) never fails {
    H2_t3:hh = H2_t3{ h: raw mkh_t3(), z: 1i64 };
    int64->:e = @hh.h.l[0i64];
    discard(raw mid_t3(@hh));
    pass (<-e) + 6i64;
};
func:f_t3 = int64(int64:n) never fails {
    H_t3:hz = raw mkh_t3(); H_t3:h0 = raw mkh_t3(); H_t3:h2 = raw mkh_t3();
    pass raw g_t3(@h0, @h2, @hz, n);
};
func:cell_main_t3 = int32() never fails { pass (((raw f_t3(1i64)) + 60i64) =>! int32); };
struct:K_t5 = { int64:k; };
struct:H_t5 = { List<int64>:l; int64:k; K_t5:kk; };
func:mk_t5 = List<int64>() never fails { List<int64>:l = raw list_init::<int64>(1i64); drop list_push(@l, 1i64); pass l; };
func:mkh_t5 = H_t5() never fails { pass H_t5{ l: raw mk_t5(), k: 2i64, kk: K_t5{ k: 3i64 } }; };
func:growh_t5 = int64(H_t5->:w) never fails { loop(0i64, 64i64, 1i64) { drop list_push(@w.l, 1i64); } pass 7i64; };
func:growl_t5 = int64(List<int64>->:l) never fails { loop(0i64, 64i64, 1i64) { drop list_push(l, 1i64); } pass 7i64; };
func:fp_t5 = int64->(H_t5->:q) never fails { pass @((<-q).l[0i64]); };
func:idh_t5 = H_t5->(H_t5->:q) never fails { pass q; };
func:getl_t5 = List<int64>->(H_t5->:w) never fails { pass @w.l; };
struct:HPO_t5 = { H_t5->:lp; List<int64>:own; };
struct:HL_t5 = { List<H_t5>:hs; int64:z; };
